== Stage 1 ==
;4 July 2026 – [[Barcelona]] to Barcelona, {{convert|19.6|km|abbr=on}} (TTT)
[[File:BCN-EstadiOlimpic-4860.jpg|thumb|The stage finished adjacent to the [[Estadi Olímpic Lluís Companys]] on the [[Montjuïc]] hill above [[Barcelona]], [[Spain]]]]
The first stage of the race was a [[team time trial]] in [[Barcelona]], [[Spain]]. The {{convert|19.6|km|abbr=on}} course started at the [[Forum Building|Fòrum]] and finished at the [[Estadi Olímpic Lluís Companys|Estadi Olímpic Louis Companys]] on the [[Montjuïc]] hill above Barcelona. Two climbs were located in the last {{convert|4|km|abbr=on}} – Côte de Montjuïc (1.1&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 5.1%) followed by the Côte du Stade Olympique (800 metres at an average gradient of 7%) to the finish line.<ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=21}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 1 - Barcelone > Barcelone - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-1 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref> The team time trial used a new format where the stage time for each team would be determined by the first rider across the finish line – each rider would receive their own time for the general classification.<ref name="CN-1"/>

{{UCI team code|NCI|2026b}} initially looked fast through the first two intermediate checks, however [[Kévin Vauquelin]] then had a puncture and [[2019 Tour de France|2019]] winner [[Egan Bernal]] was dropped. Two-time world time trial champion [[Filippo Ganna]] attacked the climbs instead of Vauquelin, with Ganna setting a time 31 seconds faster than {{UCI team code|APT|2026}}. {{UCI team code|LTK men|2026}} started faster, but [[Juan Ayuso]] slowed on the climb, eventually finishing 8 seconds slower than {{UCI team code|NCI|2026b|nolink=y}}. {{UCI team code|RBH|2026}} had both [[Remco Evenepoel]] and [[Florian Lipowitz]] attempt the final climb, however Lipowitz was dropped and Evenepoel eventually set a time 11 seconds slower than {{UCI team code|NCI|2026b|nolink=y}}.<ref name="CN-1" /><ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-04 |title=Tour de France 2026: Jonas Vingegaard in yellow after Barcelona team time trial |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/04/tour-de-france-2026-jonas-vingegaard-barcelona-team-time-trial-cycling |access-date=2026-07-06 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

{{UCI team code|TVL men|2026}} was the penultimate team to tackle the course, with four riders attempting the climbs. [[Jonas Vingegaard]] attacked and crossed the finish line 8 seconds faster than {{UCI team code|NCI|2026b|nolink=y}}. The final team to attempt the course was {{UCI team code|UEX|2026}}, but the team was slower throughout the course than {{UCI team code|TVL men|2026|nolink=y}}. In the final climb to the finish, defending champion [[Tadej Pogačar]] attacked and reduced the deficit, eventually finishing 12 seconds behind {{UCI team code|TVL men|2026|nolink=y}}.<ref name="CN-1" /><ref name=":1" />

{{UCI team code|TVL men|2026|nolink=y}} therefore took the stage win, ahead of {{UCI team code|NCI|2026b|nolink=y}} and {{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=y}}. Vingegaard took his first [[yellow jersey]] since their victory at the [[2023 Tour de France|2023]] race, and was "visibly pleased" following the stage, thanking his teammates for the effort and calling it "the perfect start". Bernal was awarded the green jersey of the points classification as he completed the first section of the course the fastest, and Pogačar was awarded the polka dot jersey of the mountains classification as he completed the final uphill section of the course the fastest. Ayuso took the lead in the young rider classification.<ref name="CN-1" /><ref name=":1" />

