== Stage 2 ==
;5 July 2026 – [[Tarragona]] to Barcelona, {{convert|168.5|km|abbr=on}}
[[File:Isaac del Toro on the podium at the presentation ceremony for Stage 5 of the 2026 Tour de France.jpg|thumb|Winner of stage 2, [[Isaac del Toro]] ({{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=}}), pictured on stage 5]]
The race remained in Spain for the second stage, with a {{convert|168.5|km|abbr=on}} course from [[Tarragona]] to Barcelona, culminating with three laps of a finishing circuit on the Montjuïc hill above Barcelona. The stage featured four categorised climbs, the second-category Côte de Begues and three ascents of the Côte du Château de Montjuïc (1.6&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 9.3%). The finish was located at the top of an uncategorised climb in Montjuïc, 700 metres in length with an average gradient of 7%.<ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=28, 31}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 2 - Tarragone > Barcelone - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-2 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref>

An breakaway of [[Frank van den Broek]] ({{UCI team code|TPP men|2026}}), [[Felix Engelhardt]] ({{UCI team code|JAY men|2026}}), and [[Alex Molenaar]] ({{UCI team code|CJR|2026}}) formed in the first {{convert|10|km|abbr=on}}. At the intermediate sprint, [[Biniam Girmay]] ({{UCI team code|NSN|2026}}) beat [[Mads Pedersen]] ({{UCI team code|LTK men|2026|nolink=y}}) and [[Jasper Philipsen]] ({{UCI team code|APT|2026|nolink=y}}). On the Côte de Begues climb, van den Broek was dropped but Engelhardt and Molenaar stayed out front until the race had entered Barcelona, with about {{convert|30|km|abbr=on}} remaining.<ref name="CN-2" /><ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-05 |title=Tour de France 2026: third stage to go ahead without spectators; Del Toro wins stage two |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/05/tour-de-france-2026-isaac-del-toro-tadej-pogacar-cycling-remco-evenepoel-jonas-vingegaard |access-date=2026-07-06 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

Entering the Montjuïc finishing circuit, the peloton gradually reduced in size thanks to a fast pace set by {{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=y}}. By the final climb, only 35 riders remained at the front, with [[Adam Yates]] ({{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=y}}) continuing the fast pace and dissuading attacks by {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders. The group stayed together, descending to the bottom on the final climb to the finish line. In the uphill sprint to the finish, Pogačar ensured that his teammate [[Isaac del Toro]] took the stage victory, with media calling the stage "a dominant ride by {{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=y}}". Evenepoel finished third ahead of Vingegaard, who maintained his lead in the {{abbr|GC|general classification}} albeit with his lead cut to 6 seconds. Del Toro took the green jersey and maintained his lead in the young rider classification. Molenaar took the lead in the mountains classification, by virtue of his day in the breakaway.<ref name="CN-2" /><ref name=":3" />

