The fourth stage of the race stayed in the Pyrenees, with a hilly course from [[Carcassonne]] to [[Foix]], with four categorised climbs (two second-category, one third-category and one fourth-category).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 4 - Carcassonne > Foix - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-4 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=44, 46}}</ref> The stage was held in "boiling temperatures", with temperatures around {{convert|40|C|abbr=on|F}}. The [[Union Cycliste Internationale]] (cycling's governing body) relaxed rules to allow riders to receive water more frequently, and increase the number of motorbikes handing out water bottles.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Moultrie |first=James |date=2026-07-07 |title='When we started, I had a full headache' – Tadej Pogačar lets Tour de France yellow jersey go to the breakaway after uncomfortably hot stage 4 |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/teams-riders/when-we-started-i-had-a-full-headache-tadej-pogacar-lets-tour-de-france-yellow-jersey-go-to-the-breakaway-after-uncomfortably-hot-stage-4/ |access-date=2026-07-10 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en |quote=boiling temperatures [..] at the start in Carcassonne, where the temperatures were pushing 40° C}}</ref><ref name=":6">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-07 |title=‘Grateful’ Træen takes yellow jersey after Pedersen wins stage four amid 40C heat |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/07/cancer-survivor-torstein-treen-yellow-jersey-pedersen-wins-stage-four-tour-de-france-cycling |access-date=2026-07-10 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> 

A breakaway of around 35 riders formed early in the stage. At the intermediate sprint, Girmay beat Philipsen and Pedersen to take maximum points, with Philipsen and Girmay dropping back to the peloton soon after. With 10 minutes between the break and the peloton, it became clear that the peloton was happy to let the break take the stage victory, and therefore teams and riders in the break attacked each other in the final {{convert|30|km|abbr=on}}. {{UCI team code|LTK men|2026|nolink=y}} chased down these attacks, setting a fast pace on behalf of Pedersen who took the stage win in a sprint finish ahead his teammate [[Quinn Simmons]] and [[Raúl García Pierna]] ({{UCI team code|MOV men|2026|nolink=}}).<ref name=":6" /><ref name="CN-4" />

The peloton finished the stage nearly 13 minutes behind Pedersen, and therefore [[Torstein Træen]] ({{UCI team code|UXM men|2026}}) – the highest placed {{abbr|GC|general classification}} rider in the breakaway – took the yellow jersey. Træen led the {{abbr|GC|general classification}} by 28 seconds over [[Sean Quinn (cyclist)|Sean Quinn]] ({{UCI team code|EFE|2026|nolink=y}}), with [[Mathias Vacek]] ({{UCI team code|LTK men|2026|nolink=y}}) 3rd overall nearly 4 minutes behind, taking the white jersey of the young rider classification.<ref name=":6" /><ref name="CN-4" /> Pogačar was 4th overall, 7 minutes 53 seconds behind, and considered that Træen would hold the jersey for a number of stages, given the margin between him and other {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Moultrie |first=James |date=2026-07-07 |title='When we started, I had a full headache' – Tadej Pogačar lets Tour de France yellow jersey go to the breakaway after uncomfortably hot stage 4 |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/teams-riders/when-we-started-i-had-a-full-headache-tadej-pogacar-lets-tour-de-france-yellow-jersey-go-to-the-breakaway-after-uncomfortably-hot-stage-4/ |access-date=2026-07-10 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en |quote=Pogačar is expecting Træen to be able to keep the jersey for some time, with such a big lead likely even allowing the Norwegian to hold onto it after the first big mountain day on stage 6}}</ref><ref name=":6" />

