== Stage 6 ==
;9 July 2026 – Pau to [[Gavarnie-Gèdre]], {{convert|186.2|km|abbr=on}}
[[File:View_from_the_top_of_the_Tourmalet_pass_at_stage_14_of_TDF_2024.jpg|thumb|View from the [[Col du Tourmalet]]]]
The sixth stage took the riders back into the Pyrenees, with a {{convert|186.2|km|abbr=on}} mountain stage from Pau to [[Gavarnie-Gèdre]], with five categorised climbs including the first-category [[Col d'Aspin]] (12&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 6.5%), the ''[[hors catégorie]]'' climb of [[Col du Tourmalet]] (17.1&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 7.3%, with the summit at an elevation of {{convert|2115|m|ft|abbr=on}}), before a summit finish at the second-category climb to Gavarnie-Gèdre (18.7&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 3.7%).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 6 - Pau > Gavarnie-Gèdre - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-6 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=60, 62}}</ref>

The weather remained hot, with temperatures around {{convert|35|C|abbr=on|F}} for the first high mountain stage of the Tour.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Farrand |first=Stephen |date=2026-07-09 |title=Tour de France enters the Extreme Weather Protocol red zone but the show will go on |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-enters-the-extreme-weather-protocol-red-zone-but-the-show-will-go-on/ |access-date=2026-07-10 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref> A three-man breakaway emerged early in the stage, with Pedersen successfully claiming maximum points in the intermediate sprint to extend his lead in the points classification. Other riders attempted to escape, as the peloton approached the large climbs of the Col d'Aspin and Col du Tourmalet.<ref name=":10">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-09 |title=Tadej Pogacar demolishes Tour de France rivals on the Tourmalet to take ominous lead |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/09/pogacar-vingegaard-col-du-tourmalet-lead-tour-de-france-stage-six |access-date=2026-07-10 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref name="CN-6" />

On the Col du Tourmalet, {{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=y}} set a fast pace, with the leading group soon less than 20 riders and Traeen in the yellow jersey losing contact {{convert|11|km|abbr=on}} from the top. With {{convert|5|km|abbr=on}} to the summit of the Tourmalet, del Toro attacked with Pogačar following. Vingegaard immediately began a solo chase, as the pace was too fast for Seixas, Evenepoel and Lipowitz. Pogačar then went solo with {{convert|43|km|abbr=on}} to the finish, summiting the Tourmalet alone, with a 31 second gap to Vingegaard and nearly 90 seconds to the Seixas, Lipowitz and del Toro group. Pogačar then descended at pace, more than doubling his advantage to Vingegaard before he began the climb to the finish at Gavarnie-Gèdre. Pogačar continued to push on the climb, taking the stage victory by 2 minutes 38 seconds ahead of Vingegaard – his largest margin of a stage win at the Tour. Six {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders (del Toro, Evenepoel, Seixas, Lipowitz, Ayuso and [[Mattias Skjelmose]] ({{UCI team code|LTK men|2026|nolink=y}})) finished a further 19 seconds behind. Pogačar therefore took the yellow jersey, with a lead of 2 minutes 42 seconds over Vingegaard, with del Toro (the new leader of the young rider classification) a further 45 seconds behind. Pogačar also took the lead in the mountains classification.<ref name=":10" /><ref name="CN-6" /> Media praised Pogačar's solo victory, calling his attack "assertive" and his lead "ominous", as well as noting that he set a new record time for the ascent of the Tourmalet by two minutes.<ref name=":10" /><ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2026-07-10 |title=Tadej Pogačar shatters Tour de France's Col du Tourmalet record ascent time by more than two minutes |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/teams-riders/tadej-pogacar-shatters-tour-de-frances-col-du-tourmalet-record-ascent-time-by-more-than-two-minutes/ |access-date=2026-07-10 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref>

On the descent from the Tourmalet, Traeen had clipped the wheel of his teammate and crashed hard. After medical attention, he continued the stage – eventually finishing around 30 minutes behind Pogačar. {{UCI team code|UXM men|2026|nolink=y}} later reported that Traeen had a "concussion and multiple rib fractures" and would not start the next day.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Giuliani |first=Simone |last2= |first2= |date=2026-07-09 |title=Torstein Træen out of Tour de France due to injuries from Col du Tourmalet crash |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/teams-riders/not-the-ending-we-wanted-for-this-yellow-adventure-torstein-traeen-out-of-tour-de-france-due-to-injuries-from-tourmalet-crash/ |access-date=2026-07-10 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":10" />

