== Stage 7 ==
;10 July 2026 – [[Hagetmau]] to [[Bordeaux]], {{convert|175.1|km|abbr=on}}

Leaving the Pyrenees, the race headed north from [[Hagetmau]] to [[Bordeaux]], on a flat course with one fourth-category climb. A sprint finish was expected.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 7 - Hagetmau > Bordeaux - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-7 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=70, 72}}</ref>
[[File:Tim Merlier on the podium at the presentation ceremony for Stage 5 of the 2026 Tour de France.jpg|thumb|Stage winner [[Tim Merlier]] ({{UCI team code|SOQ|2026|nolink=}}), pictured before stage 5]]
On the stage, Veistroffer set off in the breakaway for the second time this Tour, joined by [[Jakub Otruba]] ({{UCI team code|CJR|2026|nolink=}}). Their lead did not extend beyond 2 minutes. At the intermediate sprint with {{convert|55|km|abbr=on}} remaining, Pedersen was first of the peloton across the line, with Girmay and Kanter second and third. {{UCI team code|UXM men|2026|nolink=y}} set a high pace, and the break was swallowed with {{convert|18|km|abbr=on}} to go. Approaching the finish in Bordeaux, teams prepared their sprint trains. With {{convert|2|km|abbr=on}} to go, {{UCI team code|APT|2026|nolink=y}} moved to the front, riding for a Philipsen stage victory. However, their sprint train finished too early, ending with around {{convert|250|m|abbr=on|ft}} to the line. Merlier took advantage, accelerating to take the stage victory ahead of [[Søren Wærenskjold]] ({{UCI team code|UXM men|2026|nolink=y}}) and Girmay. Philipsen finished 5th, with Pedersen in the green jersey finishing 9th.<ref name="CN-7"></ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-10 |title=Tadej Pogacar’s Tour de France mastery leaves rivals in his wake and asking: ‘What now?’ |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/10/tim-merlier-tour-de-france-stage-seven-tadej-pogacar |access-date=2026-07-11 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077 |quote=Tim Merlier of the Soudal-Quick Step team won the seventh stage of the Tour de France in a classic sprinter’s finish on the banks of the Garonne in Bordeaux.}}</ref> There were no changes in the classifications as a result of the stage, with Pedersen leading the points classification by around 60 points ahead of Girmay and Kanter.<ref name="Stage-7">{{cite web|url=https://www.tissottiming.com/2026/tdf/stage-7/rankings|title=Tour de France – 7 – Hagetmau > Bordeaux|publisher=[[Tissot Timing]]|date=10 July 2026|access-date=10 July 2026}}</ref>

