== Stage 8 ==
;11 July 2026 – [[Périgueux]] to [[Bergerac, Dordogne|Bergerac]], {{convert|180.4|km|abbr=on}}

The eighth stage took the race from [[Périgueux]] to [[Bergerac, Dordogne|Bergerac]] over a {{convert|180.4|km|abbr=on}} flat course, with two fourth-category climbs.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 8 - Périgueux > Bergerac - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-8 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=78, 80}}</ref> 

For the second stage in a row, a sprint finish was expected and therefore the peloton did not permit the breakaway of three riders (Otruba, [[Liam Slock]] ({{UCI team code|LOI|2026|nolink=y}}) and [[Thibault Guernalec]] ({{UCI team code|TEN|2026|nolink=}})) to have more than a two minute margin. At the intermediate sprint, Philipsen beat Pedersen to the points remaining. With {{convert|30|km|abbr=on}} remaining, the gap to the breakaway had reduced to 90 seconds, with only Stock reaming out front. He rode strongly, with the gap slowly reducing as they approached the finish. With {{convert|10|km|abbr=on}} to go, Stock had a lead of around a minute, and the peloton began to push hard to chase him down. With {{convert|3|km|abbr=on}} to go, the gap was down to 10 seconds and Stock allowed himself to be captured by the peloton. Approaching the sprint finish, {{UCI team code|XAT|2026|nolink=y}} had good positioning through the final corner however Merlier had the best acceleration – beating Girmay, Kooij and Philipsen to take his second stage win in a row. Following his stage victories, Merlier moved into second place in the points classification, 15 points behind Pedersen.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Price |first=Matilda |last2= |first2= |date=2026-07-11 |title=Tour de France: Tim Merlier claims back-to-back victories on stage 8 with furious sprint into Bergerac |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-tim-merlier-claims-back-to-back-victories-on-stage-8-with-furious-sprint-into-bergerac/ |access-date=2026-07-13 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-11 |title=Heatwave forces Tour de France organisers to shorten Sunday’s stage nine |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/11/tim-merlier-back-to-back-wins-tour-de-france-cycling |access-date=2026-07-13 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> 

