== Stage 9 ==
;12 July 2026 – [[Malemort]] to [[Ussel, Corrèze|Ussel]], {{convert|154.6|km|abbr=on}}
[[File:Mathieu Van Der Poel on the podium at the presentation ceremony for Stage 5 of the 2026 Tour de France.jpg|thumb|Stage winner [[Mathieu van der Poel]] ({{UCI team code|APT|2026|nolink=}}), pictured before stage 5]]
The ninth stage of the race headed north-east from [[Malemort]] to [[Ussel, Corrèze|Ussel]], with a hilly route in the [[Massif Central]]. The stage had four categorised climbs (one second-category, two third-category and one fourth-category).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 9 - Malemort > Ussel - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-9 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=88, 90}}</ref> The stage was originally scheduled to be contested over {{convert|185.5|km|abbr=on}} but due to a red [[2026 European heatwaves#France|heatwave]] warning issued for the [[Corrèze]] department,<ref>{{cite news |date=11 July 2026 |title=Météo-France place 37 départements en vigilance rouge canicule à partir de dimanche midi, 46 en vigilance orange |trans-title=Météo-France has placed 37 departments on red alert for extreme heat starting Sunday at noon, and 46 on orange alert. |url=https://www.franceinfo.fr/environnement/meteo/bulletins-meteo/meteo-france-place-37-departements-en-vigilance-rouge-canicule-a-partir-de-dimanche-midi-46-en-vigilance-orange_8104178.html |accessdate=11 July 2026 |website=[[France Info]] |language=French |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]]}}</ref> the stage was shortened by {{convert|30|km|abbr=on}}; neither start or finish location was altered.<ref>{{cite news |date=11 July 2026 |title=Change to the route of Stage 9 as the department of Corrèze passes on red alert for a heatwave |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2026/change-to-the-route-of-stage-9-as-the-department-of-correze-passes-on-red-alert-for-a-heatwave/1341492 |accessdate=11 July 2026 |work=[[Tour de France]] |publisher=[[Amaury Sport Organisation]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Farrand |first=Stephen |date=2026-07-11 |title=Stage 9 of Tour de France shortened amid 'red alert' heatwave warning in central France |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/stage-9-of-tour-de-france-shortened-amid-red-alert-heatwave-warning-in-central-france/ |access-date=2026-07-13 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref> Some riders noted that the stage could have been held earlier in the day to reduce the high temperatures, whereas Pogačar noted that he would change the calendar to reduce the amount of racing in the summer heat of July and August.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fotheringham |first=Alasdair |date=2026-07-12 |title='I would change the whole calendar' - Tour de France leader Tadej Pogačar argues for an end to summer racing in places with intense heat |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/teams-riders/i-would-change-the-whole-calendar-tour-de-france-leader-tadej-pogacar-argues-for-an-end-to-summer-racing-in-places-with-intense-heat/ |access-date=2026-07-13 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-12 |title=Tadej Pogacar urges radical overhaul of Tour de France amid stifling heatwave |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/12/tadej-pogacar-tour-de-france-2026-heatwave-calendar-ninth-stage-mathieu-van-der-poel-cycling |access-date=2026-07-13 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

The stage was ridden in intense heat, with temperatures around {{convert|38|C|abbr=on|F}}. Due to the shortened stage, the intermediate sprint was just {{convert|13|km|abbr=on}} into the stage – Pedersen beat Girmay to take maximum points. Several attempts at a breakaway were made, but were chased back by the peloton until a group of 16 riders escaped with {{convert|95|km|abbr=on}} to go. Simmons and [[Tobias Halland Johannessen]] ({{UCI team code|UXM men|2026|nolink=y}}) decided that the break was too large and attacked on the second-category Suc au May climb, gaining 30 seconds. Others followed, with a group of 8 riders (including Simmons, Johannessen, Baudin, [[Tom Pidcock]] ({{UCI team code|PQT|2026|nolink=}}) and [[Mathieu van der Poel]] ({{UCI team code|APT|2026|nolink=y}})) summitting the climb with 75 seconds lead over the peloton.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Price |first=Matilda |last2= |first2= |last3= |first3= |date=2026-07-12 |title=Tour de France: Massive turn of power nets Mathieu van der Poel stage 9 breakaway victory just ahead of bunch sprint |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-massive-turn-of-power-nets-mathieu-van-der-poel-stage-9-breakaway-victory-just-ahead-of-bunch-sprint/ |access-date=2026-07-13 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0" />

The group of 8 worked together to try and build an advantage, however both {{UCI team code|NCI|2026b|nolink=y}} and {{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=y}} gave chase at the front of the peloton. With {{convert|50|km|abbr=on}} to go, the lead was around a minute, and the lead fell further to 30 seconds with {{convert|25|km|abbr=on}} to go. On the short but steep fourth-category climb of Mont Bressou (900&nbsp;m at an average gradient of 7.3%), van der Poel attacked, with Johannessen and Pidcock able to follow. With Baudin making the trio a quartet on the descent, they raced towards the finish with a minute lead with {{convert|15|km|abbr=on}}. {{UCI team code|NCI|2026b|nolink=y}} and {{UCI team code|LTK men|2026|nolink=y}} gave chase, but the quartet made it to the final kilometre with a small advantage over the chasing peloton. In the final sprint for the line, van der Poel was significantly faster to take his first stage win of the Tour, with Johannessen and Pidcock second and third over the line. Ganna was the first of the chasing peloton across the line, six seconds later – with Pedersen in the green jersey of the points classification behind.<ref name=":2" /><ref name=":0" />

