== Stage 10 ==
;14 July 2026 – [[Aurillac]] to [[Le Lioran]], {{convert|166.6|km|abbr=on}}

After the rest day, the race continued in the Massif Central on [[Bastille Day]], with a {{convert|166.6|km|abbr=on}} mountain stage from [[Aurillac]] to a summit finish at [[Le Lioran]]. The stage included seven categorised climbs, including the first-category climbs of [[Col du Perthus (Massif Central)|Col du Pertus]] (4.4&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 8.5%) and [[Pas de Peyrol|Puy Mary - Pas de Peyrol]] (7.8&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 6%).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 10 - Aurillac > Le Lioran - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-10 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=100, 102}}</ref>

The intermediate sprint was won by Pedersen, extending his lead in the points classification – beating Kanter, Germany and Philipsen. A breakaway went shortly afterwards, with around 30 riders up the road. The gap to the peloton was around 1 minute 40 seconds. On the Pas de Peyrol, [[Richard Carapaz]] ({{UCI team code|EFE|2026|nolink=y}}) attacked from the peloton, rapidly dismantling the breakaway on the climb. Pushing on, he had a lead of 23 seconds at the summit, and began the Col du Pertus climb with a lead of 1 minute 15 seconds ahead of the peloton as {{UCI team code|UEX|2026|nolink=y}} and {{UCI team code|DCT|2026|nolink=y}} gave chase.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last=Ostanek |first=Dani |date=2026-07-14 |title=Tour de France: Tadej Pogačar shreds the field in the Massif Central to extend GC lead and claim stage 10 |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-tadej-pogacar-shreds-challengers-with-solo-climbing-charge-on-stage-10-and-adds-more-time-to-his-advantage-with-victory/ |access-date=2026-07-24 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":7">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-14 |title=‘I have haters’: Pogacar brushes off boos after solo burst extends Tour de France lead |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/14/joy-for-tadej-pogacar-on-bastille-day-as-solo-burst-extends-tour-de-france-lead |access-date=2026-07-24 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

With a kilometre to the summit (and {{convert|15|km|abbr=on}} to the finish line), Pogačar attacked strongly. No rider could go with him, and he rode across the gap to Carapaz and passed him prior to the summit. Vingegaard and Evenepoel attempted to limit their losses, but Pogačar began the final climb to the finish at Le Lioran with a 40 second lead over the group of {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders.<ref name=":4" /><ref name=":7" />

Pogačar took his 3rd stage win of the Tour, extending his lead in the {{abbr|GC|general classification}} to over 3 and a half minutes. In the group behind, Evenepoel sprinted towards the line, finishing 32 seconds behind Pogačar and gaining a handful of seconds – allowing him to move into third overall ahead of Ayuso (who took the white jersey of the young rider classification). Del Toro fell to 7th overall, after finishing a minute behind the group of {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders.<ref name=":4" /><ref name=":7" /> 

