== Stage 11 ==
;15 July 2026 – [[Vichy]] to [[Nevers]], {{convert|161.3|km|abbr=on}}
[[File:Tour de France 2026 - Étape 11 - Juste après le départ fictif 2026-07-15 13-50-07.JPG|thumb|Neutralized start (''départ fictif'') of stage 11 in [[Vichy]]]]
The eleventh stage of the race headed north from [[Vichy]] to [[Nevers]] over a flat {{convert|161.3|km|abbr=on}} course with two fourth-category climbs.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 11 - Vichy > Nevers - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-11 |access-date=4 July 2026 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=110, 112}}</ref>

A breakaway formed after {{convert|11|km|abbr=on}}, with 4 riders including [[Mathis Le Berre]] ({{UCI team code|TEN|2026|nolink=y}}), who later took maximum points at the intermediate sprint. Philipsen won the sprint for the remaining points from the peloton. The peloton didn't give the break much time, with a small lead of around 90 seconds throughout the stage. Despite a couple of crashes in the peloton, the break was reeled in with {{convert|6|km|abbr=on}} to the finish.<ref name=":11">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-15 |title=Wærenskjold wins fastest ever Tour de France stage in frenzied sprint to Nevers |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/15/soren-waerenskjold-wins-fastest-ever-tour-de-france-stage-nevers-cycling |access-date=2026-07-24 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref name=":12">{{Cite web |last=Price |first=Matilda |date=2026-07-15 |title=Søren Wærenskjold holds off Olav Kooij to win Tour de France stage 11 |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-soren-waerenskjold-goes-from-distance-to-hold-off-olav-kooij-and-jasper-philipsen-for-victory-in-furiously-fast-stage-11/ |access-date=2026-07-24 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref>

The sprint began with {{convert|2.2|km|abbr=on}} to go, however the finish was disorganised with the lead sprinters having to work for themselves. Amid the chaos, the {{UCI team code|DCT|2026|nolink=y}} lead-out rider for Kooij – [[Cees Bol]] – had pushed on and gained a small lead over the pack. [[Søren Wærenskjold]] ({{UCI team code|UXM men|2026|nolink=y}}) took the opportunity to leap across to Bol and managed to take the stage victory over the pack of sprinters behind. Kooij was second, with Philipsen third.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":12" />

The stage was the fastest ever road stage in the history of the Tour with an average speed of {{Convert|50.9|km/h|mph}}.<ref name=":11" /> 

